  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery presents a stark contrast to 24 \nhours ago, showing mostly clear skies across eastern Idaho. \nWhile gradual cumulus development is underway over the higher \nterrain, lower elevations remain virtually cloud-free. Clear \nskies coupled with an amplifying Four Corners ridge to our south\nwill allow significant heat to return to the region. While \nafternoon highs today will remain near seasonal benchmarks in \nthe upper 80s to lower 90s across valley locations, temperatures\nwill quickly ramp up into the mid-to-upper 90s on Friday and \nlinger through the weekend into early next week. In response, a \nHeat Advisory has been issued for Friday across lower-elevation \nzones\u2014including the Snake Plain, Magic Valley, Arco Desert, \nShoshone, and the South Hills\u2014where widespread max temperatures \nof 95\u00b0F to 100\u00b0F appear likely. \n\nRegarding precipitation, an intrusion of drier mid-level air \nwill restrict overall storm coverage over the next 48 hours, \nprompting a transition back to a mix of wet and dry \nthunderstorms. High-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs)\nhighlight the Central Mountains and the Continental Divide as \nthe primary focus for isolated afternoon development today. \nConvective activity is forecast to shift southward toward the \nUtah border on Friday. \n\nA passing shortwave trough will interrupt the dry-leaning \npattern on Saturday, bringing an uptick in shower and \nthunderstorm potential. In addition to storm chances, the \ngradient will tighten, yielding widespread afternoon wind gusts \nof 30 to 40 mph across the forecast area. Behind this \ndisturbance, the broad ridge will maintain its grip over the \nIntermountain West, keeping temperatures consistently above \nnormal through early next week.",
